title: Lung cancer histology
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Key decision:'' Establish histologic subtype while conserving tissue for molecular testing before treatment selection.
* ''Adenocarcinoma:'' Often peripheral; the commonest histology in patients with little or no smoking.
** Gland formation or mucin supports diagnosis; TTF-1 and Napsin A commonly stain positive.
** Actionable oncogenic alterations are enriched, but smoking history should not restrict appropriate testing.
* ''Precursors:'' Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma in situ may appear as ground-glass nodules.
** In-situ adenocarcinoma has pure lepidic growth without invasion and measures ≤3 cm.
** Increasing solid components raise concern for invasive disease; imaging alone cannot establish histology.
* ''Squamous:'' Typically central, smoking-associated tumors; cavitation and hemoptysis are characteristic clinical clues.
** Keratinization and intercellular bridges support diagnosis; p40 and CK5/6 support squamous differentiation.
** Squamous immunostains cannot establish pulmonary origin; exclude another primary through clinical correlation.
* ''Large cell:'' Diagnose only after thorough examination of a resection excludes glandular/squamous differentiation.
** A small, undifferentiated biopsy alone cannot establish large cell carcinoma.
* ''Treatment implications:'' Pemetrexed is for nonsquamous disease; avoid bevacizumab in squamous tumors.
** Bevacizumab's pulmonary bleeding risk also makes clinically significant hemoptysis important.
* ''Sampling pearl:'' Limited TTF-1/p40 staining often preserves tissue needed for subsequent profiling.

Sources: ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 11, pp. 528–530 (PDF 535–537); NCCN Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer v7.2026, NSCL-A 1–3, Discussion MS-8–9; [[NCI NSCLC PDQ|https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/hp/non-small-cell-lung-treatment-pdq]].
